"""Session 8. Automation 2: an AI workflow (DEMONSTRATION). The same twelve customer messages as Session 1, now with an AI sort, AI reply drafts and a person who approves.

Results shown (all recorded, none live):
  sort: Session 1 AI results file, Claude, three fresh sessions given only the prompt, 4 November 2026, answer key hidden (9 of 12 match, 2 UNSURE, 1 differs: M14).
  drafts: Case Runs/prompts/F7_reply_drafts.md and runs/F7_reply_drafts_run1.md and run2.md, Claude, two fresh sessions given only the prompt, 4 November 2026, answer key hidden.
  The prompt called the five rules 'fictional'; the runs wrote their answer to a file.
Draft reading (author's own, one reader), by an explicit five-test checklist applied to every draft (slide 'My count'): a draft needs an edit if it fails any test:
  1 photo asked before any replacement or credit; 2 no delivery time, date or 'today' that Dispatch has not given; 3 payment: Finance within one working day stated;
  4 no step claimed as already done that nobody did ('We have sent/passed/asked'); 5 no action promised that no rule gives (reporting to the driver or Dispatch).
  No draft failed tests 1 or 3. 7 of 10 need an edit: run 1 M03 (5), M14 (4), M26 (4), M32 (5) = 4 of 5; run 2 M10 (2), M14 (4), M26 (4) = 3 of 5.
  Closest call kept without edit: run 1 M10 'We are taking this to Dispatch' (says what will happen, not that it has happened). Rule 5 (polite and brief) is a judgement: all ten polite; M14 drafts longest (7 and 6 sentences).
Baseline: an INVENTED example row on the Session 1 practice slide ('Practice: list ten tasks...': Sort customer messages, Daily, 45 minutes), not a measurement. Review time was not measured.
"""

